1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,990
Hello everyone and welcome back to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,210 --> 00:00:05,630
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,210 --> 00:00:11,070
And my name is Catherine and today we have an upper intermediate level lesson all about money.

4
00:00:11,510 --> 00:00:11,950
That's right.

5
00:00:12,210 --> 00:00:17,370
We are going to be talking about money but most importantly we need to start saving money.

6
00:00:17,550 --> 00:00:18,510
We need to spend less.

7
00:00:19,150 --> 00:00:19,370
Exactly.

8
00:00:19,790 --> 00:00:24,230
So, in order to control spending today we're talking about budgeting.

9
00:00:24,370 --> 00:00:27,730
So let's take a look at this very important word in today's vocabulary preview.

10
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:35,760
Alright, so as you mentioned, budgeting is, what is to budget?

11
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:36,760
To budget, that's the verb.

12
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:42,240
So budgeting is deciding how to spend your money, okay?

13
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:45,620
So that means that we prepare a plan for how to spend money.

14
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:47,940
Alright, so that can be a noun and a verb.

15
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:53,840
A budget is the plan on how you're going to spend money and to budget is to execute this plan.

16
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:55,560
Exactly.

17
00:00:55,560 --> 00:00:59,260
So, today we're budgeting a family's spending.

18
00:00:59,260 --> 00:01:02,110
We're trying to control how much this family will spend in the future.

19
00:01:02,370 --> 00:01:03,190
Alright, very good.

20
00:01:03,710 --> 00:01:06,310
So why don't we listen to this dialogue for the first time.

21
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:15,360
Welcome Mr. and Mrs. Carnwell.

22
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:15,820
Please take a seat.

23
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:16,560
Thank you.

24
00:01:17,610 --> 00:01:21,970
So, I understand that your family spending has skyrocketed and you want to start budgeting.

25
00:01:22,210 --> 00:01:23,050
Yes, that's correct.

26
00:01:23,710 --> 00:01:33,090
Frankly speaking, our household income is relatively high and we have never had any money problems, but I think this is the main reason as to why our spending has gone out of control.

27
00:01:33,730 --> 00:01:39,550
We have two kids and with allowances paying their credit cards, ours, and not to mention our mortgage and car payments.

28
00:01:40,210 --> 00:01:47,130
With the recent economic downturn, my husband's business has suffered and now we need some advice as how we can prepare a family budget.

29
00:01:47,650 --> 00:01:47,990
I see.

30
00:01:48,470 --> 00:01:50,150
Well, you have come to the right place.

31
00:01:50,870 --> 00:01:53,750
First, what we need to do is determine your cash flow.

32
00:01:54,470 --> 00:02:01,630
Knowing how much money is coming in will help us allocate spending to different categories such as mortgage, education, groceries, etc.

33
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:03,020
Yes, that makes sense.

34
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:08,600
Secondly, I need you to bring all of your receipts for the last two to three months.

35
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:14,220
That way we can determine what your average expenditures are and see which category you are spending money on the most.

36
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:23,720
Usually, your fixed costs are higher and we can't do much about that, but we can usually trim your variable costs such as entertainment or clothing.

37
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:24,720
Great!

38
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:25,240
We will do that then.

39
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:28,200
Now how about we treat you to a nice dinner?

40
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:29,950
That's another thing.

41
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:35,410
If you really want to stop spending so much money, throw away at least half of your credit cards.

42
00:02:38,770 --> 00:02:39,670
Alright, we're back.

43
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:48,110
So there were some interesting words and phrases in this dialogue related to budgeting and a lot of financial terms here.

44
00:02:48,170 --> 00:02:50,870
So let's get started now on Language Takeaway.

45
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:55,280
Language Takeaway

46
00:02:55,280 --> 00:02:59,170
This first term is very important because it tells us what kind of a budget we're talking about.

47
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:01,070
We're not talking about a company here.

48
00:03:01,240 --> 00:03:02,190
We're talking about a family.

49
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:06,240
And so in this case, we use the phrase household.

50
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:07,370
So what are we talking about?

51
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:09,440
Household income.

52
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:18,000
Alright, so income we know is the amount of money that comes in to maybe your wallet, my income, my personal income.

53
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:19,320
That's how much money you earn.

54
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:23,420
So a household income is how much money the house earns.

55
00:03:23,720 --> 00:03:26,720
So all the people that are living in this house, right?

56
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:26,840
That's right.

57
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:28,720
So it could be a family.

58
00:03:28,720 --> 00:03:30,260
It could be two married people who are married to each other.

59
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:33,200
So the idea is that it's not one person's income.

60
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:34,700
It's the household income.

61
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Think of a house.

62
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:41,520
Alright, so it's the combined income of everyone that's living there or that's making money that lives in this house.

63
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:42,520
Right.

64
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:46,240
So if Marco and I have a household, I earn $10,000 and he earns $10,000.

65
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:49,080
Our household income is $20,000.

66
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:49,290
Very good.

67
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:51,080
Alright.

68
00:03:51,080 --> 00:03:58,660
So now we know what our household income is and in this case we have children and we give our kids an allowance.

69
00:03:59,480 --> 00:04:01,960
Okay so I had an allowance when I was a kid.

70
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:05,720
This is the amount of money I got from my parents every week.

71
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:14,490
So I get I think $10 a week and I could spend it on anything I wanted and I didn't have to work because my parents gave the money to me.

72
00:04:14,930 --> 00:04:16,430
And so an allowance is this money.

73
00:04:16,630 --> 00:04:20,910
It's the money that mom and dad give to their children maybe every month or maybe every week.

74
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:24,640
Now it's not only related to children, right?

75
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:32,990
Because maybe if you have a brother or maybe you're married and your wife is a housewife, maybe you give her an allowance, right?

76
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:33,630
Or the opposite.

77
00:04:33,930 --> 00:04:37,070
Maybe you're a house husband and you get an allowance from your wife.

78
00:04:37,350 --> 00:04:37,730
That's right.

79
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:39,230
You can have an allowance if you're anyone.

80
00:04:39,560 --> 00:04:43,640
You can also have an allowance from your company if you go traveling.

81
00:04:43,640 --> 00:04:46,690
So if you have a business trip, your company gives you a food allowance.

82
00:04:47,230 --> 00:04:48,850
That's the amount of money you can spend on food.

83
00:04:49,190 --> 00:04:50,310
And you don't have to work for it.

84
00:04:50,410 --> 00:04:50,990
That's the best part.

85
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:52,520
Awesome.

86
00:04:52,520 --> 00:05:02,000
Alright so after these allowances, they were talking about how the economic downturn has affected their income and their spending.

87
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,900
Okay so down, you think this is a negative thing.

88
00:05:05,080 --> 00:05:05,320
You're right.

89
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:11,800
Downturn means when a situation gets worse or slows down.

90
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:14,040
And in this case, the economy is what got worse.

91
00:05:14,280 --> 00:05:15,580
And you remember this from last year.

92
00:05:16,160 --> 00:05:19,400
Things started to get very difficult for people who are in business.

93
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:21,160
And this has affected families.

94
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:25,360
And so we call this situation an economic downturn.

95
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:28,220
Now the opposite would be an economic boom.

96
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:29,260
That's right.

97
00:05:30,040 --> 00:05:31,980
A boom is when things are going well.

98
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:34,080
That means people are earning more money.

99
00:05:34,300 --> 00:05:35,120
There are more jobs.

100
00:05:35,280 --> 00:05:36,280
Alright.

101
00:05:36,280 --> 00:05:36,460
Very good.

102
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:45,890
Now moving on, as they were talking about budgeting, they mentioned this very important word, very technical, is to allocate money into different areas.

103
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:47,560
That's right.

104
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:47,990
Allocate spending.

105
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:49,560
This is a verb.

106
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:50,560
To allocate.

107
00:05:50,560 --> 00:05:53,570
That means to decide where the money will go.

108
00:05:54,470 --> 00:06:00,770
Okay so as the mother in a family, I will allocate 10% of the money to the children's allowances.

109
00:06:01,520 --> 00:06:01,790
That's a lot.

110
00:06:02,870 --> 00:06:04,610
50% to the mortgage.

111
00:06:05,170 --> 00:06:06,470
20% to the car.

112
00:06:07,320 --> 00:06:07,920
Car repairs.

113
00:06:08,360 --> 00:06:10,200
So you're allocating your spending.

114
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:11,200
That's right.

115
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:11,960
I'm deciding where to spend the money.

116
00:06:13,240 --> 00:06:16,900
And well, talking about spending, we have this word, expenditures.

117
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:20,200
Expenditures.

118
00:06:20,200 --> 00:06:28,780
And so this is a really important word if you're in business as well because we consider expenditures when we're talking about how much a company earns.

119
00:06:28,780 --> 00:06:31,360
Expenditures are things that we spend money on.

120
00:06:31,560 --> 00:06:32,980
So remember that word, expenditures.

121
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:34,800
Spend.

122
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:35,800
Okay.

123
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:36,800
So an expenditure.

124
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:39,360
That's a noun and usually it's in the plural.

125
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:40,360
Expenditures.

126
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:40,700
Exactly.

127
00:06:41,020 --> 00:06:41,440
Expenditures.

128
00:06:42,360 --> 00:06:45,240
So we have many expenditures at my house.

129
00:06:45,240 --> 00:06:49,120
Among them, we have the car, the rent, healthcare, things like that.

130
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:50,520
Okay.

131
00:06:50,520 --> 00:06:51,520
Perfect.

132
00:06:51,520 --> 00:06:53,000
So really good phrases there.

133
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:54,760
Really good vocabulary items.

134
00:06:55,400 --> 00:06:57,440
Why don't we move on now to Fluency Builder.

135
00:06:59,160 --> 00:06:59,420
Fluency Builder.

136
00:07:00,160 --> 00:07:02,820
We've got some very fun phrases today, Marco, in Fluency Builder.

137
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:08,920
This first one is a very common phrase that we hear in songs and movies.

138
00:07:08,920 --> 00:07:09,380
To skyrocket.

139
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:10,920
To skyrocket.

140
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:11,920
All right.

141
00:07:11,920 --> 00:07:12,920
What is skyrocket?

142
00:07:12,920 --> 00:07:15,820
Well, you think about a rocket, you know, that's a ship that goes to space.

143
00:07:15,820 --> 00:07:19,880
To skyrocket means to go up really high, really fast.

144
00:07:21,050 --> 00:07:23,290
The price of gas has skyrocketed.

145
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:28,640
So I remember when I was a kid, gas used to be 98 cents a gallon.

146
00:07:28,640 --> 00:07:35,670
And in the 2000s, the price skyrocketed and it ended up being like $4 a gallon.

147
00:07:35,830 --> 00:07:36,770
Yeah, it was really expensive.

148
00:07:37,490 --> 00:07:37,690
All right.

149
00:07:38,200 --> 00:07:43,890
So this is also, you can talk about sales have skyrocketed or the price of petroleum has skyrocketed.

150
00:07:44,080 --> 00:07:47,330
So it could be a good thing or a bad thing, depending on what you're talking about.

151
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:47,750
All right.

152
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:51,910
And now moving on, they said that their spending was out of control.

153
00:07:52,240 --> 00:07:53,240
Yeah.

154
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:55,230
So this family doesn't seem to understand how to budget.

155
00:07:55,560 --> 00:07:57,130
They spend on everything.

156
00:07:57,740 --> 00:08:02,980
And so the accountant says that your spending is out of control.

157
00:08:02,980 --> 00:08:04,390
That means that you're spending too much.

158
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:07,070
There's no order to the way that you're spending.

159
00:08:07,480 --> 00:08:15,650
And we actually have a lesson, a previous lesson called out of control spending that we also touched this topic of what we talk about out of control.

160
00:08:16,280 --> 00:08:16,890
Something is out of control.

161
00:08:17,280 --> 00:08:18,470
A person can be out of control.

162
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:19,270
Like a child.

163
00:08:19,550 --> 00:08:20,830
This child is out of control.

164
00:08:21,110 --> 00:08:23,630
He never stops screaming and hitting people.

165
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:25,080
All right.

166
00:08:25,080 --> 00:08:30,050
And our last phrase for today, the accountant says, well, you've come to the right place.

167
00:08:30,650 --> 00:08:30,970
Okay.

168
00:08:31,050 --> 00:08:31,650
This is him.

169
00:08:31,910 --> 00:08:33,310
This is the accountant bragging.

170
00:08:33,810 --> 00:08:37,030
He's saying, well, I can fix your problems.

171
00:08:37,310 --> 00:08:37,970
I can help you.

172
00:08:38,150 --> 00:08:39,350
You've come to the right place.

173
00:08:40,070 --> 00:08:41,510
You can use this in many different ways.

174
00:08:41,510 --> 00:08:49,610
For example, I have a problem maybe with my TV and I say, Marco, oh my gosh, my TV doesn't work.

175
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:50,470
I don't know what to do.

176
00:08:50,740 --> 00:08:51,030
Can you help?

177
00:08:53,070 --> 00:08:53,210
Right.

178
00:08:53,640 --> 00:08:54,640
And I can say, well, you've come to the right place.

179
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:55,640
I'm an expert in my TV.

180
00:08:55,640 --> 00:08:56,590
Marco's an expert in electronics.

181
00:08:56,640 --> 00:08:57,640
I'm an expert.

182
00:08:57,750 --> 00:08:58,090
All right.

183
00:08:58,110 --> 00:08:59,710
So, you've come to the right place.

184
00:08:59,850 --> 00:09:07,250
It's a very common phrase, especially as you say when somebody's trying to brag and say, well, I know how to fix your problems or I know what you need.

185
00:09:07,730 --> 00:09:08,590
I'm good at this.

186
00:09:09,010 --> 00:09:10,230
You've come to the right place.

187
00:09:10,230 --> 00:09:12,390
If you want to learn English, you've come to the right place.

188
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:13,640
Exactly.

189
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:14,640
EnglishPod.com.

190
00:09:14,640 --> 00:09:15,640
All right.

191
00:09:15,640 --> 00:09:24,600
So, let's listen to our dialogue for the last time.

192
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:26,280
Welcome Mr. and Mrs. Carnwell.

193
00:09:26,280 --> 00:09:26,760
Please take a seat.

194
00:09:27,280 --> 00:09:27,480
Thank you.

195
00:09:28,530 --> 00:09:32,910
So, I understand that your family spending has skyrocketed and you want to start budgeting.

196
00:09:33,130 --> 00:09:33,970
Yes, that's correct.

197
00:09:34,610 --> 00:09:39,430
Frankly speaking, our household income is relatively high and we have never had any money problems.

198
00:09:40,090 --> 00:09:44,030
But I think this is the main reason as to why our spending has gone out of control.

199
00:09:44,610 --> 00:09:50,490
We have two kids and with allowances, paying their credit cards, ours, and not to mention our mortgage and car payments.

200
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:58,060
With the recent economic downturn, my husband's business has suffered and now we need some advice as how we can prepare a family budget.

201
00:09:58,580 --> 00:09:58,920
I see.

202
00:09:59,340 --> 00:10:01,040
Well, you have come to the right place.

203
00:10:01,760 --> 00:10:04,680
First, what we need to do is determine your cash flow.

204
00:10:05,380 --> 00:10:12,540
Knowing how much money is coming in will help us allocate spending to different categories such as mortgage, education, groceries, etc.

205
00:10:12,980 --> 00:10:13,940
Yes, that makes sense.

206
00:10:14,580 --> 00:10:18,680
Secondly, I need you to bring all of your receipts for the last two to three months.

207
00:10:19,220 --> 00:10:25,160
That way we can determine what your average expenditures are and see which category you are spending money on the most.

208
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:29,920
Usually, your fixed costs are higher and we can't do much about that.

209
00:10:29,920 --> 00:10:33,860
But we can usually trim your variable costs such as entertainment or clothing.

210
00:10:34,520 --> 00:10:35,520
Great!

211
00:10:35,520 --> 00:10:36,180
We will do that then.

212
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,120
Now how about we treat you to a nice dinner?

213
00:10:40,280 --> 00:10:40,880
That's another thing.

214
00:10:41,280 --> 00:10:46,240
If you really want to stop spending so much money, throw away at least half of your credit cards.

215
00:10:51,560 --> 00:10:52,560
Alright we're back.

216
00:10:52,560 --> 00:10:56,040
So now talking about allowances, you said you had an allowance when you were a kid.

217
00:10:56,040 --> 00:10:57,490
Did you really have a $10 a week allowance?

218
00:10:57,720 --> 00:11:00,000
No, I think it was like $8.

219
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:05,550
It started out at $6 or $5 a week and then I think the most I got was like $10 or $11.

220
00:11:06,530 --> 00:11:08,630
So it was like almost about a dollar a day.

221
00:11:09,190 --> 00:11:09,590
About that.

222
00:11:09,750 --> 00:11:17,870
But usually the point was that, well, I started working when I was 15 because my parents thought it was important for children to learn how money works.

223
00:11:19,400 --> 00:11:24,590
But I was given that money because at the time it was enough money to go out one night.

224
00:11:24,950 --> 00:11:33,050
So on Friday nights if I wanted to go out with my friends, I could go out to watch a movie and get a slice of pizza and $10 was enough.

225
00:11:33,770 --> 00:11:36,910
Now $10 doesn't even buy you a ticket to the movie.

226
00:11:37,220 --> 00:11:39,160
But at the time it was more than enough.

227
00:11:39,190 --> 00:11:47,890
So this is important I guess because different families have different ways of upbringing their children and in many cases this doesn't happen.

228
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:51,720
If I want to go out to the movies on a Saturday, then my parents will give me $30.

229
00:11:51,720 --> 00:11:53,630
And if I want to go out next weekend, they'll also give me money.

230
00:11:53,880 --> 00:12:01,960
So it's interesting to see the contrast because obviously when I was a kid also I didn't really get this opportunity of going out all the time.

231
00:12:01,960 --> 00:12:03,470
And actually I had to work for it.

232
00:12:03,570 --> 00:12:09,870
So if I wanted to go out on the weekend, I would get an allowance but also I would have to wash the car, that was typical with my dad.

233
00:12:10,070 --> 00:12:10,950
I had to wash the car.

234
00:12:11,330 --> 00:12:15,950
Well, I think it's interesting because it shows that the parents want to teach their children about budgeting.

235
00:12:16,560 --> 00:12:19,670
Because if you only have $10, you really have to budget.

236
00:12:19,960 --> 00:12:23,780
You have to decide how you will allocate that money even though it's not very much.

237
00:12:24,040 --> 00:12:25,660
So in some ways it's good practice.

238
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:26,440
Yeah, it is.

239
00:12:26,760 --> 00:12:27,640
I think it's done us well.

240
00:12:27,780 --> 00:12:28,300
We're not in debt.

241
00:12:28,660 --> 00:12:29,560
Not yet.

242
00:12:30,380 --> 00:12:31,020
All right.

243
00:12:31,280 --> 00:12:36,020
So let us know how allowances work in your country or maybe did you have an allowance when you were a kid?

